---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-privy';
const title = 'Klaviyo vs Privy for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Klaviyo and Privy for Shopify capture, email and SMS campaigns, purchase exits, operating workload and contribution after costs.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'When is Klaviyo a good fit?', answer: 'Consider Klaviyo when your team needs email and SMS coordinated around customer profiles and behavioral segments. Validate the event timing, plan access and staff effort with your actual Shopify data.' },
  { question: 'When is Privy a good fit?', answer: 'Consider Privy when on-site capture, email and SMS campaigns match your store’s daily work. Its current offering includes automations and segmentation; test the specific branches and exclusions you need.' },
  { question: 'Is Privy only a popup tool?', answer: 'No. Privy currently offers email and SMS campaigns and automations as well as displays. A displays-only plan can also feed selected external platforms, including Klaviyo.' },
  { question: 'What does the Sendlane acquisition change?', answer: 'Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane in February 2026. Confirm your account’s product access, migration path, sender setup and support arrangements; an acquisition announcement does not establish feature parity or automatic migration.' },
  { question: 'Can I use Privy capture with Klaviyo?', answer: 'Privy lists Klaviyo among supported destinations for captured contacts. Test field mapping, channel-specific permission and update latency before relying on that handoff for scheduled campaigns.' },
  { question: 'Do I need another email or SMS provider?', answer: 'Both products offer email and SMS. Add another provider only for a demonstrated requirement, with one owner for each flow and tested suppression across systems. A separate email layer is not a default requirement.' },
  { question: 'How should I test purchase exits?', answer: 'Queue a cart reminder, then place an order before delivery. Check both channels and any external sender, including a delayed event and retry, to verify that the queued reminder is retired rather than merely excluding the next campaign.' },
  { question: 'Which costs should the comparison include?', answer: 'Compare the same eligible audience, email volume, destination mix and SMS segments. Include contact tiers, credits, overages, carrier charges, duplicate tools, implementation and operating time using current written quotes.' },
  { question: 'Are the merchant scenarios reported results?', answer: 'No. They are proposed test fixtures for distinct merchant jobs. Their outcomes must be measured in your store; they are not customer evidence or performance promises.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Klaviyo vs Privy</nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Shopify campaign comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Privy for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around the work your team must run: capturing a useful preference, coordinating email and SMS, and stopping an obsolete message before it reaches a buyer. Both products offer email and SMS; neither needs a separate sender simply because of its category.</p>
      </header>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Where each product can fit</h2>
        <p>Klaviyo is a candidate when customer-profile data and behavioral segmentation drive your campaigns and your team can maintain the flows. Privy is a candidate when capture, campaigns and automation form a manageable daily workflow for your operators. These are conditional fits, not a claim that one product is limited to capture or that the other automatically improves margin.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Klaviyo</th><th>Privy</th><th>Proof to request</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Email and SMS</td><td>Offers both on a shared platform</td><td>Offers campaigns and automations for both</td><td>Run the same two-channel flow in your account</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Capture</td><td>Evaluate forms alongside profile data</td><td>Offers displays and a displays-only option</td><td>Confirm preference and permission mapping</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Candidate for behavior-driven orchestration</td><td>Current pricing lists targeting and segmentation</td><td>Rebuild your actual exclusions and measure refresh lag</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating fit</td><td>Test the work to maintain branching flows</td><td>Test capture-to-campaign work and support handoff</td><td>Time setup, revisions and incident recovery</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <h2>Current product scope and access</h2>
        <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing">Klaviyo email</a> and <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/products/sms-marketing">Klaviyo SMS</a> describe coordinated messaging. <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy pricing</a> lists email, an SMS add-on, automations, segmentation and displays. Privy also lists selected external capture destinations, including Klaviyo. Confirm countries, sender registration, plan entitlements and integration behavior for your account.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its Sendlane acquisition</a> on February 2, 2026. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> describes email, SMS and on-site tools together. Ask which interface and capabilities your account receives, whether a migration is needed, and who owns support. Do not infer that every legacy Sendlane feature or sender configuration is automatically available in every Privy account.</p>
        <h2>Proposed merchant fixtures</h2>
        <p>These fictional planning scenarios preserve distinct jobs worth testing. Names identify fixtures only; no sales totals, conversion gains or merchant endorsements are claimed. Compare each on a matched eligible audience, and record failures as well as orders.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick — holiday gifting:</strong> capture gift versus self-purchase intent; send bundle reminders to gift shoppers and scent education to self-purchasers. Test shipping-cutoff expiry and purchase exits.</li>
          <li><strong>Arena Energy — athlete flavor launch:</strong> separate launch timing from a shaker incentive. Verify that email redemption prevents a duplicate SMS offer.</li>
          <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame — limited prints:</strong> disclose edition size and target interested repeat collectors. Trigger a scarcity reminder only from fresh inventory, never a preset claim.</li>
          <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co — seasonal restock:</strong> use editorial email for surf content and a variant-specific waitlist for restock alerts. Exclude buyers and unavailable sizes.</li>
          <li><strong>Noir Collective — timed streetwear drop:</strong> schedule VIP access before general release. Compare a restrained calendar against the existing cadence without inventing a frequency benefit.</li>
          <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX — sneaker priority:</strong> preserve size preference and waitlist order; expire early links and cancel queued alerts when that size sells out.</li>
          <li><strong>Festival Threads Co — tour merchandise:</strong> capture city preference separately from shipping address. Check local scheduling and retire a city campaign after its event.</li>
          <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records — collector access:</strong> define repeat-order eligibility and exclusions in advance. Test members who also joined a pressing waitlist so they receive one access message.</li>
          <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear — clearance:</strong> distinguish bargain-sensitive shoppers from full-price loyalists. Use a holdout to measure contribution from the offer.</li>
          <li><strong>Sauce Lab — small batches:</strong> propose one launch and one inventory-dependent reminder. Cancel the reminder if inventory is stale or the batch is gone.</li>
          <li><strong>Ember Ceramics — scarce kiln release:</strong> preserve queue priority and available-piece reservations. Keep maker-story email distinct from the access notification.</li>
          <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee — subscriptions:</strong> exclude active subscribers from acquisition flash offers. Test a subscription status change after a message has been queued.</li>
          <li><strong>Cipher Audio — high-value launch:</strong> combine education with a relevant engaged audience. Compare a no-discount launch with an offer using net contribution, not attributed revenue.</li>
          <li><strong>Patchwork Denim — one-of-one listings:</strong> capture style and size preferences, then check item availability immediately before dispatch. A sold listing must retire all related jobs.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Capture, identity and permission acceptance test</h2>
        <p>Use test profiles for a new visitor, an existing email contact adding a phone number, a shared phone, a changed email address and an opted-out customer. Store channel-specific permission, source, time and the disclosure actually shown. A captured preference or an email signup does not establish SMS permission. Keep historical opt-outs when merging identities; do not treat a fresh import as fresh permission.</p>
        <p>Submit a Privy form, then inspect the intended destination profile and fields. If Klaviyo owns delivery, verify the handoff before activating its flow. Test duplicate submissions, a failed sync and a delayed update. Resolve ambiguous identities before sending; a successful integration connection alone does not prove correct eligibility.</p>
        <h2>Purchase exits, queued jobs and incident ownership</h2>
        <p>Queue a cart reminder and a launch message, then purchase, unsubscribe, change subscription status or sell out the selected variant. Re-evaluate permission, purchase state, inventory and campaign expiry at dispatch. Retire obsolete queued jobs explicitly; a new segment exclusion may leave an older scheduled job alive.</p>
        <p>Replay the same event and simulate a timeout after provider acceptance. Use a stable event or job key to prevent duplicate retries; reconcile uncertain delivery status before resending. Check delayed and out-of-order purchase events, timezone boundaries and quiet-hour rescheduling. Recheck eligibility after any delay.</p>
        <p>Assign one person to investigate failures and one system to own each flow. The handoff record should include profile identity, permission evidence, event time, queue state, sender response and resolution. If a required control needs external automation, include its cost and owner in the comparison. Pause the affected flow until failures are understood.</p>
        <h2>Matched cost and contribution worksheet</h2>
        <p>Obtain current quotes from <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo</a> and <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy</a>. Match eligible contacts, email sends, destination countries, SMS encoding and billable segments, peak traffic and support scope. Privy distinguishes mailable-contact email pricing, SMS credits and page-view-based displays; verify the selected configuration rather than comparing starting prices.</p>
        <p>Include base fees, credit or usage charges, carrier charges not already included, overages, extra tools, migration and staff time. Avoid counting bundled charges twice. Model both normal and peak months and growth in the billable audience.</p>
        <p>Proposed arithmetic fixture: randomly assign 1,000 eligible customers to treatment and 1,000 to holdout. Suppose net sales after discounts and returns are $6,000 and $5,000, while variable product and fulfillment costs are $2,400 and $2,000. Incremental contribution before messaging is ($6,000 − $2,400) − ($5,000 − $2,000) = $600. If incremental messaging and operating costs are $200, the pilot contributes $400, or $0.40 per treatment customer. These inputs are hypothetical, not expected results.</p>
        <p>Do not subtract discounts or returns again from already-net sales. For unequal groups compare contribution per eligible customer before scaling the difference. Add recurring platform and staffing costs when comparing the full operating alternatives; distinguish those from marginal pilot costs. Track complaints, opt-outs and refunds over a suitable observation window alongside contribution.</p>
        <h2>Choose after the pilot</h2>
        <p>Choose Klaviyo if the tested profile and flow work justifies its cost and maintenance for your team. Choose Privy if its tested capture, email and SMS workflow covers the required jobs with acceptable effort and contribution. Privy displays feeding Klaviyo can also fit when capture is a distinct need, provided the handoff passes. Add another provider only for a documented gap and retest every cross-system exit.</p>
        <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission guide</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> to prepare the pilot. Compare the <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy</a> profiles for additional context. Sending rules depend on jurisdiction, recipient and message type; verify applicable requirements and account controls before launch.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Privy FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
